{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat AGENTS.md && sed -n '70,125p' docs/02-product-spec.md && sed -n '148,177p' docs/02-product-spec.md && sed -n '165,205p' docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md && sed -n '288,309p' docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md && sed -n '365,380p' docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Working in this repository\n\nAxylo is a browser charting library. The product is specified in `docs/02-product-spec.md`; the build plan is `docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md`. Read the sections your task names before writing code.\n\n## Rules for every change\n\n- **Contracts are fixed.** `packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts` and `packages/axylo/src/public.ts` are the interfaces between modules. Do not edit them. If your work needs a change there, stop that part, finish what you can, and describe the exact change you need in your report.\n- **Stay in your files.** Each task lists the directories it owns. Do not create, edit or delete anything else, including configuration and other modules. Several agents work in this checkout at the same time.\n- **No runtime dependencies.** Use the platform (`Intl`, typed arrays). Dev dependencies already installed: TypeScript, Vitest, fast-check, esbuild, Playwright (system Chrome), axe-core. The network is not available; do not try to install anything.\n- **No DOM at import time.** A module that needs the DOM takes it as a parameter or touches it inside a function. Pure stages (data, scales, formatting, layout, scene) must run in Node.\n- **Hot paths use typed arrays.** No object per data point, no array of arrays per data point, no copying of large columns that the contracts say are borrowed.\n- **Never guess silently.** Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported through the `DiagnosticSink` with the code from spec section 3.5.\n- **Tests come from the spec.** Expected values are derived from the specification and the fixture data, never by running the implementation and pasting its output. Use fast-check property tests for invariants (\"for any data …\"). A test you cannot make pass is reported as failing; do not weaken it or change its expected value.\n- **TypeScript strict, no `any`** in exported signatures. `__DEV__` is a build-time boolean (true in tests).\n- **Comments say why**, not what. Names are plain words.\n- **Do not commit.** The coordinator commits.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nnpm run typecheck      # tsc over the library, its tests, and the API examples in docs/api\nnpm test               # unit and property tests (Node)\nnpm run build          # packages/axylo/dist\nnpm run test:browser   # browser tests against the built bundle, in system Chrome\n```\n\n## Your report\n\nEnd with a short report: what you built (files), what you verified and how (paste the last lines of the test run), what is not done or not verified, any contract change you need, and any place where the spec was ambiguous and what you assumed. Keep \"ran and saw\" apart from \"expect\".\n| W2 | Fill a dashboard | Dozens of charts per page; container-sized; themed by the page; loading, empty and error states; a chart can take its name from the card's own heading. |\n| W3 | Compare categories | Long labels; horizontal bars; grouped and stacked. |\n| W4 | Watch a live metric | Append without redraw cost growing; a pause control; no announcement flood. |\n| W5 | Explore a range | Tooltip anywhere, legend toggle, zoom and pan by single click, drag, touch and keyboard. |\n| W6 | Read it another way | Keyboard, screen reader, table view, high contrast, reduced motion, 400% zoom, color-vision deficiency. |\n\n### 2.3 Supported environments\n\n| Dimension | 1.0 |\n|---|---|\n| Browsers | The current and previous major versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari (macOS and iOS). |\n| TypeScript | 5.4 and later. Plain JavaScript is fully supported; types are an aid, not a requirement. |\n| React | 18 and 19, through `@axylo/react`. |\n| Text direction | Left-to-right pages. Right-to-left text renders, but the layout is not mirrored; this is stated in the conformance table. |\n| Built-in text | English, overridable (A10). |\n\n## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\n\nEvery criterion maps to at least one test in the acceptance matrix kept in the repository (criterion → test → chart types it applies to → result). \"Property test\" means the statement is checked over at least 1,000 generated datasets. Expected values come from the fixture data and this specification, never from the implementation's output.\n\n### 3.1 Faithful representation\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | Bar and area value axes always include zero. An author `domain` that excludes zero is widened and raises AX110. | Property test on domains, including all-negative and mixed-sign data; `bars` fixture (values 82–97) starts at 0 | R1 |\n| F2 | Missing values are never drawn as zero and never bridged unless the author asks. An explicit `null` is a gap, silently. An isolated value between two gaps is a visible point. A missing bar shows a \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. On a time or date axis with a regular step, an absent step is a gap and raises AX105; `missing: 'zero'` on the field turns absent and null values into zeros for counts. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". | `trend` with nulls and `trend` with the same 7 rows deleted render the same gap; `dirty` fixtures; property test that no segment connects across a missing index | R5 |\n| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\n| F4 | Three temporal types, never mixed silently. **Calendar dates** (`2026-02-24`, `2026-02`, `2026`) read the same in every viewer time zone. **Instants** (epoch milliseconds declared `type: 'time'`, `Date` objects, ISO strings with an offset) are shown in `timeZone` (default: the viewer's), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the description. **Wall-clock times** (date-time strings without an offset) are shown as written, with hint AX112. Instants that all fall on the same time of day, whole days apart, raise hint AX111 (\"these look like calendar dates\"). A bare number is never assumed to be an instant. | Four fixture variants in UTC, America/Los_Angeles and Asia/Kolkata: (a) date strings: identical text in all three; (b) ISO strings with `Z`, (c) epoch ms with `type: 'time'`, (d) `Date` objects: each zone's correct local text, zone visible, AX111 raised; with `timeZone: 'UTC'` or `type: 'date'`: identical. Plus: both DST transitions, a leap day, an unparseable date | R3 |\n| F5 | No smoothing. When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified with this guarantee: for every device-pixel column, and every contiguous run of valid samples inside it, the drawn stroke covers exactly that run's minimum and maximum. A break between two runs is always kept, even when both fall in one pixel column; a slot a series merely has no row for (weekly data beside daily) does not break its line. Stacked areas are simplified on their cumulative sums. Exact values always come from the data, not the drawing. | Assertions on which source samples the drawing kept, for: one outlier in 1,000,000; two peaks and a gap in one column; alternating valid and missing samples; tied extremes; device pixel ratio 1 and 2; several zoom levels. A sample that the drawing dropped is still reachable by keyboard and in the table | D8, D9 |\n| F6 | Numbers and dates are formatted for the page locale. Ticks and readouts are formatted separately: ticks may be compact (`50K`); tooltip, table and announcements show the full integer part, and the fraction with up to six significant digits not counting the fraction's leading zeros (`123,456.75`; `0.000123456`; `0.1 + 0.2` reads `0.3`), with the unit. A custom formatter that throws falls back to the default and raises a diagnostic. | Expected strings for chosen values (large integers, small decimals, percentages, zoned times) in `en-US`, `de-DE` and `en-IN` | R4 |\n| F7 | Domains follow one order of precedence: the zoomed view is inside the author's `domain` if given, otherwise inside the automatic domain. The automatic domain contains all data, reference lines and stack totals, and ends at the first tick at or beyond each extreme. A field declared `scale: 'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1) ends at full scale only when its maximum is at least half of full scale and not above it. Nothing is inferred from a label. | Property tests; `bars` ends at 100 when declared `percent`; a 4% maximum ends at the first tick at or above 4% | R1 |\n| F8 | Donut: slices keep the given order, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. The legend shows every slice's value and percentage as text, in slice order. A zero slice is listed without an arc; a `null` slice is listed as \"No data\". A negative value is AX120 and the chart shows its error state. With more than eight slices, the seven largest are drawn and the rest are grouped as \"Other (n)\", with AX121; the table lists every category. An all-zero donut shows the empty state. | `parts` fixture; one test per case | R7, landscape scoreboard 3.1 |\n| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker, and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\n| F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \"incomplete\" in tooltip, table and announcements. | Property test on stacked geometry; mixed-sign and incomplete fixtures | R5 |\n| F11 | Line, area and scatter are drawn in x order whatever the input order. On line, area and bar, two rows with the same x in one series are AX108 and that series is not drawn. On scatter, points sharing an x (or both coordinates) are ordinary data. | Shuffled `trend` renders identically; duplicate fixture | R5 |\n| F12 | When a warning means values were left out or changed, the chart says so to the reader: a short note (\"Values not shown: 2. See table.\", worded so it reads correctly for any count) inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, in every build. | Production build given `dirty` shows the note | R5, R6 |\n\n### 3.2 Legibility\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L1 | No visible text overlaps other text or is clipped, and no label disappears without the reader being told. Remedies, in order: thin (time, date, numeric and `ordered` category axes only); wrap to at most three lines; rotate up to 45°; truncate in the middle, keeping every label distinct, with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, a visible note (\"Some labels hidden. See table.\") appears and AX143 suggests horizontal bars. Axylo never changes a chart's orientation on its own. | In real browsers, after fonts load: for widths 240–1,920 px and generated label sets, zero intersecting text boxes (1 px tolerance), zero text outside the figure, truncated labels pairwise distinct. Repeated with the WCAG 1.4.12 text-spacing overrides, 200% text-only zoom, and a font that loads late | R2 |\n| L2 | Text is at least 12 CSS px and at least 4.5:1 against what is behind it, including text drawn inside a mark and legend entries for hidden series. Marks, axis lines and focus indicators are at least 3:1. Gridlines are exempt: they are not needed to read a value while the tooltip and table exist. Holds in light and dark. | Computed-style and rendered-pixel checks in both schemes | R7, R10 |\n| L3 | The chart follows its container with no call from the page, in both directions. With no definite height, the plot is 16:9, clamped between 160 and 480 CSS px tall, and the title, controls and table flow above and below it. A container that starts hidden or zero-sized draws when it gets a size. | Resize 720 → 360 → 720 px; hidden-then-shown test; `height: auto` container is stable over 10 frames | R16, D1 |\n| L4 | Light or dark is taken from the chart element's computed `color-scheme`; if that is not set, from the luminance of the inherited text color. `--axylo-background` overrides both. Marks repaint within one frame when the scheme, forced colors, contrast preference, or the `class`, `style` or `data-theme` of `<html>` or `<body>` changes. Forced-colors mode uses system colors. | A page that sets `color-scheme`; a class-toggled dark theme without it; a light-only page on a dark device (must stay light); a runtime toggle; forced-colors emulation | R10 |\n| L5 | A marker on every point appears only when adjacent points are at least 12 px apart (provisional value), and always for isolated points. | `trend` at 720 px has none; a 12-point line has them | R8 |\n| L6 | Title, subtitle and axis titles with units are part of the chart. A value axis is titled with its label (the field name when none is given) and unit. An x axis that is a measure (number) is titled the same way; a time, date or category x axis is titled only when the author gives a `label`, since its ticks already say what it is. A reference line or band shows its label next to it. Axis titles never overlap tick labels. | Layout tests; `trend` fixture | R9 |\n| L7 | Empty data shows a message inside the plot area; so do loading, error and Axylo's own internal failures. No invented axis ranges, no blank box. A change of state is announced once, politely. | `empty` fixture: message visible and in the accessibility tree, no tick labels; forced internal error shows the error state | R6 |\n| L8 | When the figure cannot hold a readable plot (less than 120×80 CSS px for the plot), it shows the title, the one-line summary and the table control instead, and raises AX142. | Narrow and short container tests | R2 |\n\n### 3.3 Interaction\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| I1 | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the picking rule for the chart type (section 3.6), within one frame at the 95th percentile. On line, area and bar the tooltip lists every visible series at the nearest x; a series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\", distinct from \"No data\". The tooltip stays inside the viewport, does not cover the active mark, can itself be hovered, and lists at most eight rows plus \"+N more\". When it cannot fit, the same content appears in a fixed readout under the plot. Escape closes it until the pointer reaches a different datum. | Hover at 20 positions including all four edges; sparse-series fixture; narrow tile with eight series; Escape test | R15 |\n| I2 | Legend entries are toggle buttons with a pressed state, operable by click, Enter and Space. | Keyboard and accessibility-tree tests | R12 |\n### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\n\nLevel decides delivery. **Error**: the chart or a series cannot be drawn; the chart shows its error state. **Warning**: something is not drawn as given; the reader sees the note in F12. **Hint**: advice for the author. Errors and warnings print once per code per page in every build; hints print in development builds only. `strict: true` throws on errors and warnings found while validating a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. A diagnostic that can only arise later (a contrast check after the page's CSS changes, a custom formatter that throws when a table page is built, a label that stops fitting after a resize) is reported when it is found and is never thrown. Every diagnostic names the chart; those about data name the field and the first row indexes in the caller's own data.\n\n| Code | Level | Condition | What is drawn |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AX100 | Error | Typed columns have different lengths | Error state; the message names the columns and their lengths |\n| AX101 | Error | A named field is not a key of any row | Error state; the message lists the keys that exist and the closest match |\n| AX102 | Warning | A field is absent (`undefined`) in some rows | Gaps |\n| AX103 | Warning | A value is `NaN` or infinite | Gap |\n| AX104 | Hint | Every non-missing value of a value field is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`) | Parsed and drawn; the message suggests `parse: 'number'`. An empty string counts as missing |\n| AX105 | Hint | Steps are absent on a regular time, date, wall-clock or number axis | Gaps. When a series would need more absent steps than twice its observations (and more than 1,000), it is treated as irregular instead: no gaps are created and the hint says so |\n| AX106 | Warning | A value field mixes numbers with strings, or holds strings that are not plain decimals (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`) | The strings are not drawn |\n| AX107 | Warning | A date or time cannot be parsed | That row is not drawn |\n| AX108 | Error | Duplicate x within a series, or duplicate donut labels | That series, or the donut, is not drawn |\n| AX109 | Hint | Numbers on an axis declared `time` look like seconds, or a numeric x looks like epoch milliseconds without `type: 'time'` | As declared |\n| AX110 | Warning | An author domain on bar or area excluded zero | Domain widened to zero |\n| AX111 | Hint | Instants look like calendar dates | As instants, zone shown |\n| AX112 | Hint | Date-time strings have no offset | As written |\n| AX120 | Error | Negative donut value | Error state |\n| AX121 | Warning | More than eight donut slices | Seven largest plus \"Other (n)\" |\n| AX130 | Warning | More than eight series | All series, with dash or marker differences |\n| AX140 | Warning | No `title` and no `labelledBy` | Generated name |\n| AX141 | Warning | Palette below 3:1 on the resolved background | As themed |\n| AX142 | Hint | Plot too small to draw | Summary and table control |\n| AX143 | Hint | Labels hidden after every remedy | Note shown to the reader |\n| AX150 | Warning | Unknown option key | Ignored; the message suggests the closest known key |\n| AX151 | Warning | `append` rows out of order | Accepted through the slower full update |\n| AX131 | Hint | A compact chart has more than one series | Drawn, with dash differences |\n| AX132 | Hint | More bars than pixels along the category axis | Drawn; the message suggests aggregating |\nThe adapter is prototyped in M3, before the API freezes, because it is the cheapest way to find out whether the core contract survives a second consumer. It ships in 1.0 because finding 1.1 of the landscape shows where everyday charts are written; that is evidence of where charts are written, not of demand for Axylo.\n\n## 2. Architecture\n\n### 2.1 Pipeline\n\n```\noptions ─► normalize ─► frame ──┬─► view model ─► layout ─► scene ─► mark renderer\n            │ validate   (authoritative) │   scales, visible range         text renderer (DOM)\n            ▼ diagnostics                ├─► lookup: exact values by x, by point, by ordinal\n                                         ├─► summary and table\n                                         └─► focus model\n```\n\nThe **frame** is the normalized, lossless data: typed columns, a validity bitmap, dictionary-encoded categories, source-row indexes and a revision number. It is the only source for exact values. The table, the summary, keyboard navigation, tooltips and events read the frame. The **scene** is geometry for drawing and may be simplified (F5); nothing reads values back from it. The first draft derived accessibility from the scene, and two reviewers showed that a simplified drawing cannot return the samples it dropped.\n\n| Stage | Output | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| normalize | Frame, diagnostics | The only place raw input is read. Never coerces silently. |\n| view model | Scales, visible index ranges, series visibility | Domain rules implement F1 and F7. |\n| lookup | Nearest-x by binary search; nearest-point by a grid index (scatter) | Works on the frame within the visible range; cost bounded independently of the drawing. |\n| layout | Plot rectangle, tick sets, label boxes, legend and control placement | Implements L1, L3, L8. Takes a text-measurement interface as input. |\n| scene | Flat list of primitives (`path`, `rect`, `arc`, `rule`, `text`) | Plain data; testable in Node with injected text metrics. |\n| renderers | Marks; DOM text; accessibility DOM | See 2.3. |\n\n### 2.2 Invalidation\n\nStages are cached, and the cache keys are explicit. Caching on object identity alone, as the first draft said, would miss font loads, pixel-ratio changes and in-place mutation.\n\n| Stage | Reruns when |\n|---|---|\n| normalize | Data revision or field encoding changes. `append` extends the frame without renormalizing earlier rows. |\n| view model | Frame revision, zoom, series visibility, author domains. |\n| layout | View model; container size; resolved font and text spacing; `document.fonts` finishing a load; locale; any label text. A data-only update reruns layout when the tick labels would change, and skips it otherwise. |\n| scene | Layout or view model. |\n| mark raster | Scene; device pixel ratio; resolved colors (L4's triggers). |\n\nText is measured with the page's computed font, letter spacing and word spacing applied to the measuring context, and layout reruns when fonts finish loading. The measurement interface is a parameter, so Node tests inject fixed metrics and browser tests use the real one.\n\n### 2.3 Rendering\n\nOutcomes:\n- One test per diagnostic code; the combined and one-bad-value dirty fixtures yield exactly the codes in the spec.\n- F4's four fixture variants in three zones, plus DST and leap-day cases.\n- Property tests: no scene path contains a non-finite coordinate; no segment crosses a missing index; domains obey F7.\n- A production build given `dirty` shows the reader's note.\n\nExample: \"What Axylo tells you about your data\". Docs: Diagnostics reference, one page per code; Dates and time zones.\n\n### M3. Reading the chart (ceiling: 10 days)\n\n- Title, `labelledBy`, subtitle, axis titles; toolbar with legend toggles; full tooltip and fixed readout (I1); label fitting (L1) and the too-small fallback (L8); states (L7); reference lines and bands.\n- React adapter prototype, used to test the commit contract (1.5, 1.7).\n\nOutcomes:\n- L1's property test in three engines, including text-spacing overrides and a late-loading font, on a category-axis line chart with long labels.\n- Tooltip tests at the edges, with sparse series, and in a narrow tile.\n- `empty`, loading, error and internal-error states visible and in the accessibility tree, each announced once.\n- Adapter tests: prop removal, handler replacement, inline field objects, StrictMode.\n\nExample: \"A dashboard tile with a target line and an empty state\". Docs: Titles and labels, Tooltip, Legend, States.\n\n### M4. Accessibility, complete on the line chart (ceiling: 10 days)\n\nOutcomes: every acceptance-matrix row is pass or not applicable with a reason; changelog, upgrade policy and third-party notices published.\n\nPublishing to npm, tagging and pushing are separate, explicit decisions by the owner at this point; the plan does not assume them.\n\n## 4. Test strategy\n\n| Layer | Tool | What it proves |\n|---|---|---|\n| Unit | Vitest | Scales, ticks, formatting, parsing, simplification. |\n| Property | fast-check | Invariants that must hold for any data: F1, F2, F5, F7, F11, L1. |\n| Scene snapshot | Vitest, injected text metrics | Geometry and text for each fixture and width, without a browser. |\n| Browser | Playwright on three engines | Real text boxes, resize, pointer, touch, keyboard, time zones, color schemes, reduced motion, fonts loading late. |\n| Accessibility | axe-core, accessibility-tree snapshots, keyboard scripts, recorded rehearsal | A1–A10. |\n| Visual | Screenshots per operating system | What the scene cannot see: antialiasing, state leaks. |\n| Types | `docs/api/examples.ts` pattern | The listed mistakes fail to compile (DX3). |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
